.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR expr_vwritef[t][_r]()
function write the string formatted under
.IR fmt [ fmtlen ]
to
.I fd
by
.IR writer .
The arguments are fetched from
.I addr
by
.IR arg.
.P
The
.BR expr_writef[t][_r]()
function fetch the argument from
.IR args [ arglen ].
.P
The
.BR expr_[v]apwritef[t][_r]()
function fetch the argument from
.IR "... " or " ap" .
.P
The
.BR expr_[v]snprintf[t][_r]()
function write the formatted string to
.IR buf [ buflen ].
.P
The
.BR expr_[v]asprintf[t][_r]()
macro write the formatted string to a space with enough size and let
.IR *strp
point to it. The space must be deallocated by
.I mtl
->
.B deallocate()
when it is no longer needed.
.P
The
.BR expr_snwritef[t][_r]() , expr_aswritef[t][_r]()
behaves like
.BR expr_snprintf[t][_r]() , expr_asprintf[t][_r]()
but fetch the argument from
.IR args [ arglen ].
.P
The
.BR expr_mkinstf[t][_r]()
function generate an array of type
.B struct expr_finst
and write the address of it to
.IR *fip .
The space of the array must be deallocated by
.B expr_ifree[_r]()
when it is no longer needed.
.P
The
.BR expr_*i[_r]()
function use
.I fip
to format the string instead of
.IR fmt [ fmtlen ].
.SH DEFAULT FORMAT
Defined by
.IR expr_ftable_default .
.P
"%[flags][width][.digit][:argsize](conversion|control)"
.P
width, digit, argsize can be "*", to read from argument(width,argsize:size_t. digit:ssize_t), or be "**" to read from the pointer provided by argument.
.SS FLAGS
.TP
.B #
Append a 0/0x to the start of a nonzero octal/hex number.
.TQ
.B +
Append a + to the start of a positive number.
.TQ
.B 0
Pad with zeros instead of whitespaces.
.TQ
.B -
Left justification.
.TQ
.B ' '
Append a whitespace to the start of a positive number if '+' not set.
.TQ
.B =
Stop if no more effective digits.
.TQ
.B ?
Use the saved value instead read from arguments.
.TQ
.B &
Read the value from the pointer provided by argument.
.TQ
.B !
Set cap explicitly.
.SS ARGSIZE
.TP
.B number
Use the number as size.
.TQ
.B empty
expr_t
.TQ
.B p
pointer
.TQ
.B z
size_t
.TQ
.B c
char
.TQ
.B h
short
.TQ
.B i
int
.TQ
.B l
long
.TQ
.B q
long long
.TQ
.SS CONVERSION
.TP
.B %
% .
.TQ
.B s
Standard string.
The length will be get by
.B strlen()
if
.I digit
is not set, or
.B strnlen()
if
.I digit
is set.
.TQ
.B S
String with length
.IR digit .
.TQ
.B f
double.
.TQ
.B d
.RB "signed integer(" expr_it ")."
.TQ
.B u 
.RB "unsigned integer(" expr_ut ")."
.TQ
.B x
.RB "unsigned integer(" expr_ut "), in hex."
.TQ
.B o
.RB "unsigned integer(" expr_ut "), in octal."
.TQ
.B b
.RB "unsigned integer(" expr_ut "), in binary."
.TQ 
.BR m , M
.RB "unsigned integer(" expr_ut "), in human readable format."
.TQ
.B c
Character.
.TQ
.BR h , H
Hexdump from the pointer provided by argument the with length
.IR digit .
.TQ
.BR E
Escaped string from the pointer provided by argument the with length
.IR digit .
.TQ
.B p , P
Address of a pointer.
.SS CONTROL
.TP
.B n
Write the number of written bytes to the address of the pointer provided by argument. The object length of the pointer depends on
.IR argsize .
.TQ
.B l
Repeat the next conversion for
.I width
times.
.TQ
.B L
Like
.B l
but fetchs the next argument before each times.
.TQ
.B T
Set the delim between two looped conversions or array elements.
Use
.RI p[ width ]
as the content, when p points to the next character of 'T'.
.TQ
.B t
Set the tail of each looped conversion or array elements.
Use
.RI p[ width ]
as the content, when p points to the next character of 't'.
.TQ
.B k
Delcare the next conversion is for an array
.IR argument [ width ],
when the
.I argment
points to the first element of the array.
The size of each elements will be get from the
.I argsize
of the conversion, or be overrided with
.IR digit .
.TQ
.B @
Use the character from argument for conversion or control.
.TQ
.B z
Flush the buffer if
.I fd
refers to an
.B struct expr_buffered_file
object. Or drop the contents in the buffer if
.B #
is set.
The behavior is undefined if
.I fd
does not refer to an
.B struct expr_buffered_file
object.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR expr_[v||vap|ap]write[f[t]|i][_r]()
return the number of bytes written. On error the negative code or
will return. On truncate
.B SSIZE_MIN+128
will return.
.P
The
.BR expr_[v]snprintf[t][_r]() , expr_[v]snwritef[t][_r]()
return the number of bytes should be written to
.IR buf " (excluding the terminating zero)"
in the case that the space is enough. The return value that >=
.I buflen
means the output was truncated.
.P
The
.BR expr_[v]asprintf[t][_r]() , expr_aswritef[t][_r]()
return the number of bytes written to
.IR *strp " (excluding the terminating zero)."
.RI "On fail, negative value will rerurn(and " *strp " will be undefined)."
.P
The
.BR expr_mkinstf[t][_r]()
return the number of elements in
.IR *fip .
.RI "On fail, negative value will rerurn(and " *fip " will be undefined)."
.SH WARNING
The
.IR fmt [ fmtlen ]
for
.BR expr_mkinstf[t][_r]()
should not be changed or freed before
.I *fip
be freed, or the behavior will be undefined.
